  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\n===Patchy Fog May Bring Slick Spots on Roads===\n\nWinds are calm to light and skies are nearly clear across the region \nat this hour. Due to these conditions, temperatures have dropped \ninto the mid 30s. Temperatures are expected to continue falling into \nthe upper 20s and low 30s by sunrise. Aloft, the upper troughing \ncontinues to pinwheel through the lower Ohio Valley and there is \nanother shot of vorticity moving south from central Indiana this \nmorning. This lift and narrow moisture layer has developed some \nlower-level clouds. These clouds will continue to push south and \nexpand into the morning hours. Given these early morning conditions, \nit is possible that we will see some patchy fog development. \nTherefore, there may be some slick spots on the roads (especially \nelevated roads) this morning. \n\nDuring the day today, upper troughing will begin to push northeast \nand surface high pressure will build in from the northwest. Residual \nmoisture in the low levels will keep scattered to broken skies \nthrough most of the day (especially over the Bluegrass region). \nIncreasing heights aloft, ample mixing, and sunshine will allow high \ntemperatures to warm into the upper 40s and low 50s. \n\nTonight, winds will be light and skies mostly clear (except the \nBluegrass). With ample radiational cooling, expect to see lows in \nthe mid-upper 20s for most and low 30s in the Bluegrass. Patchy fog \nwill be possible early Sunday morning. Given below freezing \ntemperatures, areas of fog may bring slick spots on roads. Anyone \ntraveling early Sunday morning should use caution. \n\n===Dry and Warming Through Next Week===\n\nRidging will be featured over the central Plains on Sunday and will \ngradually push east and amplify through mid next week. At the \nsurface, high pressure will gradually move into the Ohio Valley and \nintensify, reaching near 1036mb mid next week. This setup will allow \nfor temperatures to warm 3-5 degrees each day through mid next week, \nreaching the mid-upper 60s. Calm and clear nights will allow for \nefficient radiational cooling, therefore, lows will be in the 20s \nand 30s. \n\nLate next week, troughing will move through the Great Lakes region \nand force upper ridging and surface high pressure to push to the \neast. Conditions will remain dry through this period, though \ntemperatures will continue to warm into the upper 60s and possible \nthe low 70s.",
